  "query_preview": "..Thursday morning sees the beginning of a stronger \natmospheric river move into the area. Some rather impressive \ndynamics look to be associated with this storm, with strong \ninitial warm air advection, and relatively cold air advecting \neastward. These ingredients coupled with sea surface temperatures \nstill around the mid 50s means that the environment leans more \ntowards the convective. Furthermore, a strong jet aloft with \nplenty of divergence behind the warm front sets the stage for meso\nvertices forming along the front. These cause a number of issues:\ntiming, location, and intensity of rainfall and highest winds can\nshift drastically. Individual model runs tend to struggle with \nthese environments, with run to run consistency of both the GFS \nand EC inconclusive on individual features. Ensemble guidance \nprovides a clue on the location of highest wind speeds and \nrainfall however. At this point, the southern half of the \npanhandle has the highest likelihood of longer duration, heavier \nrainfall. The specifics regarding the hydrology portion of this \nstorm are noted in the hydrology section. \n\nLooking towards the weekend, there is roughly a 60-40 split of\nactive weather continuing and a stronger low passing south\nrespectively. If active weather continues, then generally\nsoutherly winds will accompany mostly rain for the panhandle. For\nthe low passing south, this would invite stronger, downsloping\nnortherlies, which would dry out the panhandle and bring cooler\ntemperatures overnight.",
